<h1>Orcraft Test Step</h1>
<p>A step is the smallest unit of work in Orcraft: one action, its inputs, its outputs. Steps exist for
  two reasons. They make a long case body readable by naming its parts, and they are the only component
  that can be dispatched to a remote endpoint - which is what turns a local test into a distributed
  one.</p>

<p>Like every component, a step is three classes:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from __future__ import annotations

import os

from orcraft import (
	OrcraftTestStep,
	OrcraftTestStepConfig,
	OrcraftTestStepResult,
	OrcraftConfigField,
	OrcraftResultField,
)


class MyStepConfig(OrcraftTestStepConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Step")
	output_folder = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="", label="Output Folder")
	run_id = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="", label="Run Id")


class MyStepResult(OrcraftTestStepResult):
	output_file = OrcraftResultField.Text(default="", label="Output File")


class MyStep(OrcraftTestStep):
	def __init__(self, config: MyStepConfig | None = None) -> None:
		super().__init__(config or MyStepConfig())

	def _run_step(self) -> OrcraftTestStepResult:
		self.result = MyStepResult()

		output_file = os.path.join(self.config.output_folder, f"{self.config.run_id}.dat")
		self.result.output_file = output_file

		was_removed = os.path.exists(output_file)
		if was_removed:
			os.remove(output_file)

		self.checkpoint(
			True,
			f"Stale output removed: {output_file}" if was_removed else "No stale output to remove.",
			"",
		)

		return self.result') }}

<h2 id="run-step">Step body</h2>
<p><code>_run_step</code> is the only method a step must implement. It creates its result, does the work,
  and returns the result - and the returned object must be an <code>OrcraftTestStepResult</code>, which
  the framework checks rather than letting a mistyped return surface later as a missing attribute.</p>

<p>A step is not handed a case. That is the point: it receives everything through its config and reports
  everything through its result, with no access to the test around it. Keeping to that discipline is what
  lets the same step run locally during development and on a remote endpoint in a distributed run,
  unchanged.</p>

<h2 id="config-fields">Config fields</h2>
<p>Every field a step config starts with, before you add your own. Only <code>target_endpoint</code> and
  <code>expect_endpoint_disconnect</code> are specific to steps; the rest is the base every component
  shares:</p>

<h3 id="config-code">Overridable in code</h3>
<p>Set in your config class, fixed for the run.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>name</code></td>
      <td><code>"Step"</code></td>
      <td>Display name in the GUI, CLI and logs. Not editable from the GUI. The framework prefixes it with <code>Step: </code> in the log.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>description</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Free text describing what the step does. Not editable from the GUI.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td><code>HORIZONTAL</code></td>
      <td>Orientation of the logged config table, or <code>OFF</code> to leave it out of the log.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Override carefully</span>
  <p>Redeclare a field with the same <code>OrcraftConfigField</code> type and a new default -
    <code>name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Step")</code>. A bare assignment such as
    <code>name = "My Step"</code> is not an override: it shadows the field with a plain attribute, and the
    GUI, the log table and <code>required</code> validation no longer see it.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="config-reserved">Reserved</h3>
<p>Written by the framework while a run is in progress.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>target_endpoint</code></td>
      <td>The endpoint the step is dispatched to. Set by the flow that runs it, not usually by hand.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>expect_endpoint_disconnect</code></td>
      <td>Marks a step that is <em>supposed</em> to take its endpoint offline - a reboot, a service restart - so the disconnection is not reported as an error.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>start_time</code></td>
      <td>When the run started. Stamped by the framework at run time - leave it alone.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_tab</code></td>
      <td>Log indentation. Assigned by the framework at every level - it cannot be overridden, only collided with.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_title_color</code>, <code>log_title_bg_color</code></td>
      <td>Palette plumbing for the log banners. Not user-facing.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--caution">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Do not redeclare</span>
  <p>A field of your own under one of these names replaces the framework's. The framework keeps writing
    to the name, but through your definition - and the log indentation, the run timestamps and the
    endpoint a step is dispatched to silently stop meaning what the controller expects.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="step-result-fields">Result fields</h2>
<p>A step creates its result inside <code>_run_step</code> and returns it. The fields you declare on it
  come from the <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/result-fields/"><code>OrcraftResultField</code></a>
  namespace; the rest it inherits from <code>OrcraftTestStepResult</code>, and most of those are written
  by the framework rather than by you:</p>

<p class="docs-legend"><span class="mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</span> overridable by user (optional)
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <span class="mark mark--framework">{% icon "cpu" %}</span> written by the framework, may still be set directly
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <span class="mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</span> reserved</p>

