<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Override carefully</span>
  <p>Fields you may set are assigned at run time - <code>self.result.warning_description = "..."</code> -
    not redeclared. Redeclare only to change a default, and then with the same
    <code>OrcraftResultField</code> type:
    <code>log_orientation = OrcraftResultField.Format(default=OrcraftResultField.LogTableOrientation.VERTICAL)</code>.
    A bare value on the class shadows the field, and the log table and the controller no longer see it.</p>
</div>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--caution">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Do not redeclare</span>
  <p>A field of your own under any of these names replaces the framework's. The framework keeps writing
    to the name, but through your definition - and status comparisons, the calling flow's <code>handle_errors</code>, and the controller's result columns silently break. Only
    <code>log_orientation</code> is meant to be overridden, and only to change its default.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="granularity">Granularity</h2>
<p>A useful step is one action that can succeed or fail on its own terms: delete a file, launch a process,
  read a register, transfer a build. If a step needs two checkpoints about unrelated things, it is
  probably two steps. If it exists only to pass a value from one step to the next, it is probably not a
  step at all.</p>

<p>Splitting at that grain pays off twice. The log gains a banner and a result table per action, so a
  failure points at the action that produced it, and each of those actions becomes independently
  reusable - the same step appears in several
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/flows/">flows</a> across different tests.</p>

<h2 id="remote">Remote execution</h2>
<p>When a flow runs a step against a remote endpoint, the framework transfers the test package to that
  endpoint, executes <code>_run_step</code> there, and returns the result to the controller. The step
  code does not know the difference, which means two things in practice:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Paths inside a step are paths on the machine the step is executing on. Anything a step needs must
    arrive as a package field or through the flow's
    <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#transfer">transfer</a> helper.</li>
  <li>Anything a step wants to hand to the next step must go through its result, since the two may not be
    running on the same machine. It arrives with its Python type intact - see
    <a class="text-accent" href="#type-fidelity">Type fidelity</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p><a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/">Orcraft Endpoints</a> covers dispatch, package upload and
  the endpoint field types in full.</p>

<h2 id="type-fidelity">Type fidelity</h2>
<p>A flow passes values between steps as if every step ran in the same process, and the framework keeps
  that true across endpoints. Arguments are pickled to the endpoint and results are pickled back; lists,
  tuples and dicts are walked so nested structures keep their shape; enums and your own classes work
  because the test package is on the endpoint too. A tuple arrives as a tuple, not a list, and an enum
  member arrives as that member, not its string.</p>

{{ codeblock.block('first_result = self.run_step(
	MyProduceStep,
	endpoint=self.config.endpoint_1,
)

if self.handle_errors(first_result, self.result):
	return

# channel_map is still a tuple, mode still an Enum member, calibration still a dict of
# floats - even though first_result came from endpoint_1 and MyConsumeStep runs on endpoint_2.
second_result = self.run_step(
	MyConsumeStep,
	endpoint=self.config.endpoint_2,
	channel_map=first_result.channel_map,
	mode=first_result.mode,
	calibration=first_result.calibration,
)') }}

<p>This is what lets a flow be written once and distributed later: the code that wires one step's output
  into the next step's input does not change when the steps move to different machines. The values
  usually travel as <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/result-fields/#plain-attributes">plain
  attributes</a> on the result and <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#plain-attributes">plain
  attributes</a> on the next config, since structured data is rarely something a person wants as a GUI
  column or input.</p>

<h3 id="remote-objects">Non-transferable values</h3>
<p>Some values have no meaning off the machine that made them - a thread, an open socket, a process
  handle. When a remote step puts one of those on its result, it does not come back as a value. It
  stays on the endpoint, and the flow receives a <em>reference</em> to it instead. That reference can be
  handed to a later step on the <em>same</em> endpoint, which gets the live object back; passing it to a
  local step, or to a different endpoint, raises. That keeps a long-lived resource usable across several
  steps without pretending it can be copied.</p>

<h2 id="standalone">Direct execution</h2>
<p>Steps are normally run by a flow through
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/flows/#run-step"><code>run_step</code></a>, which builds the
  step, applies the config values and dispatches it. A step can also be constructed and run directly,
  which is occasionally useful while developing one:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('step = MyStep(
	MyStepConfig(output_folder="/tmp/run", run_id="0001")
)
result = step.run()') }}

<p>A step run this way always runs locally - remote dispatch is something the flow arranges.</p>
